      "updated": 1790425137185,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The Consistency Spectrum\n\nConsistency is not a single property. It's a spectrum from strongest to weakest. Understanding the spectrum is more useful than knowing CAP,\nbecause different parts of your system will have different requirements.\n\nFrom strongest to weakest:\n\nLinearizability (Strict Consistency)\n        │\n        ▼\nSequential Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nCausal Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nRead-Your-Writes Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nMonotonic Read Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nEventual Consistency\n\n                                                        LINEARIZABILITY(The Strongest — CAP's \"C\")\n\nThis is the absolute gold standard of consistency. It creates the perfect illusion of a single database.\n\nThe Core Rule: It is strictly bound to physical real-world time. Once a write operation is confirmed as successful, no client can ever read the \nold data again, regardless of which server on earth they query. Operations appear to execute instantaneously.\n\nThe Analogy (The Global Whiteboard): Imagine a whiteboard in New York magically linked to boards in Tokyo and London. If I change the New \nYork board from \"$100\" to \"$50\", nobody in Tokyo or London is allowed to look at their boards until the ink is perfectly dry on all three.\n\nThe Cost (The Latency Penalty): To achieve this, the primary node uses synchronous replication. It locks the record, sends the update to \nreplicas, and holds the user's connection open until all replicas confirm. My database's write speed is literally capped by the latency of the \nslowest, laggiest server in the cluster.\n\nThe Google Spanner Exception: Standard databases struggle to do this globally because physical server clocks naturally drift out of sync. \nGoogle solved this by building the TrueTime API. They installed atomic clocks and GPS receivers directly into their data center racks. \nTrueTime gives Spanner a guaranteed \"maximum uncertainty window\" (e.g., ±4 milliseconds). By intentionally pausing for those few milliseconds \nbefore returning a success response, Spanner mathematically guarantees a perfect global chronological order without the brutal latency penalties \nof cross-continental locking.\n\nWhen to use it: High-frequency stock trading, or any system where a millisecond difference in the real world dictates a massive financial outcome.\n\n                                                    SEQUENTIAL CONSISTENCY (The Pragmatic Transaction Model)\n\nThis model throws away the physical clock entirely. It does not matter when events actually occurred in the real world; it only matters that all \nnodes agree on a single, official sequence of events.\n\nThe Core Rule: As long as every process in the system agrees on the exact same numbered sequence of operations, the system is sequentially \nconsistent. Different nodes might lag behind real-time, but they will never process events out of the agreed-upon order.\n\nThe Analogy (The WhatsApp Group Chat): Alice asks who is driving. Bob and Charlie reply at the exact same real-world millisecond. The server \nreceives Charlie's packet first and decides the order is Alice → Charlie → Bob. Even if Dave has a terrible 3G connection and doesn't get the \nmessages for 10 seconds, his phone will display them in that exact top-to-bottom order. Everyone agrees on the history.\n\nHow it works without clocks: Instead of relying on timestamps, distributed systems use consensus algorithms like Raft or Paxos. These \nalgorithms force the nodes to vote and create an official ticket number for every transaction.\n\nWhy it is perfect for standard transactions: If a user makes a deposit and a withdrawal at roughly the same time, I don't need TrueTime to \nkeep the math safe. The consensus algorithm votes: Deposit is Ticket #1, Withdrawal is Ticket #2. Even if a specific replica is lagging and \nreceives the withdrawal network packet first, it looks at the ticket number and refuses to execute it until it receives and processes Deposit #1. \nIt guarantees \"First In, First Out\" based on the official list, fully protecting the ledger.\n\n                                                                                       CAUSAL CONSISTENCY\n\nIf operation A causally precedes operation B (A happened before B, or A's result influenced B), then all nodes see A before B. Unrelated opera-\ntions can be seen in any order.\n\nThe analogy: A comment thread. If User A posts a comment and User B replies to it, everyone should see User A's comment before User B's reply \n(because the reply causally depends on the original). But if User C posts an unrelated comment at the same time, it doesn't matter whether C's \ncomment appears before or after A's comment on different nodes.\n\nWhere it's used: Social media comment ordering. Git — commits that build on previous commits must appear in order. Google Docs collaborative \nediting — your edits that build on previous state must be applied in order.\n\nVector clocks (covered later in this distributed systems section) are the mechanism for tracking causality. They're what makes causal consistency \nimplementable.\n\n                                                                    READ-YOUR-WRITES CONSISTENCY\n\nAfter you write something, you always see your own write in subsequent reads. Other users might not see it yet (it's still propagating), but you \nalways see your own writes.\n\nThe analogy: You post a tweet. You immediately refresh and see your tweet. Your friend in another country might not see it yet — the data \nhasn't propagated to their closest server. But you always see your own tweets immediately.\n\nHow it's implemented:\n\nOption 1: Read from the same node you wrote to (sticky routing)\n    - Write goes to Server A\n    - All reads for this user go to Server A (until replication catches up)\n    - Problem: Server A becomes the bottleneck for this user\n\nOption 2: Track write timestamps\n    - After writing, client receives a \"write token\" with timestamp\n    - On reads, client sends the token\n    - Read replica checks: \"Is my replication lag less than this timestamp?\"\n    - If yes, serve from replica. If no, route to primary.\n\n                                                                       MONOTONIC READ CONSISTENCY\n\nOnce you've read a value, you'll never read an older value. You won't go backward in time.\n\nThe analogy: Reading a news website. You read the headline \"Stock market up 3%.\" If you refresh, you might see \"Stock market up 3.2%\" (newer) \nbut you'll never see \"Stock market up 2%\" (older than what you already saw).\n\nWithout monotonic reads: You could read from Replica A (latest data), then your next request goes to Replica B (which is slightly behind), and \nyou see older data. This creates a disorienting time-travel effect.\n\nHow it's implemented: Session-based routing — once a user reads from a specific replica, all subsequent reads for that session go to the same \nreplica (or a replica that's at least as up-to-date).\n\n\n                                                            EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY (The Weakest)\n                                                            \nIf no new writes occur, eventually all replicas will converge to the same value. But in the meantime, different replicas might return different values \nfor the same key.\n\nThe analogy: Airport departure boards. When a flight status changes, the update propagates from the central system to all displays — but for a \nbrief window, some displays might show \"On Time\" while others show \"Delayed.\" Eventually they all show the same thing.\n\nThe key property: \"Eventually\" has no time bound specified. It could be milliseconds, seconds, or minutes. The system makes no guarantee about \nHOW eventually.\n\nWhere it's acceptable:\n- DNS propagation (24-48 hours is acceptable)\n- Social media like counts (seeing 1,423 vs 1,424 likes doesn't matter)\n- Product recommendation caches (slightly stale recommendations are fine)\n- Shopping cart contents (debatable — Amazon famously uses eventual consistency for carts)\n- Analytics data (approximate counts are usually acceptable)\n\nWhere it's NOT acceptable:\n- Bank balances (must be consistent — can't show £1,000 to one teller and £500 to another)\n- Inventory counts (can't oversell because replicas showed different availability)\n- Authentication/authorization (can't let a revoked token still work on some nodes)\n- Financial ledgers (every transaction must be correctly sequenced)\n\n                                                              THE PRACTICAL DECISION FRAMEWORK\n\nIs incorrect data dangerous or just inconvenient?\n    Dangerous (money, auth, inventory) → Strong consistency (linearizability or sequential)\n    Inconvenient (stale cache, slightly old feed) → Eventual consistency\n\nWhat's the write frequency?\n    High-frequency writes, read-heavy → Eventual consistency (replicas can't keep up synchronously)\n    Low-frequency writes → Strong consistency is feasible\n\nWhat's your latency requirement?\n    Sub-10ms → Eventual consistency (synchronous replication adds too much latency)\n    100ms+ acceptable → Strong consistency is viable\n\nIs your data a social graph or interconnected relationships?\n    Yes → Causal consistency (replies must appear after their parent comment)\n    No → Simpler model sufficient\n\nDo users care about their own writes immediately?\n    Yes → At minimum, read-your-writes consistency\n\n\n                                                                    HOW THESE RELATE TO CAP\nCAP's \"C\" = Linearizability (the strongest model)\nCP systems → Linearizability or Sequential Consistency\nAP systems → Causal, Read-Your-Writes, Monotonic, or Eventual Consistency\n\nBut most real systems don't just pick one:\n- Cassandra: Eventual by default, tunable to Sequential with CONSISTENCY ALL\n- DynamoDB: Eventual by default, strong with ConsistentRead=true (costs 2x read units)\n- MongoDB: Eventual by default, strong with majority read concern",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The Consistency Spectrum\n\nConsistency is not a single property. It's a spectrum from strongest to weakest. Understanding the spectrum is more useful than knowing CAP,\nbecause different parts of your system will have different requirements.\n\nFrom strongest to weakest:\n\nLinearizability (Strict Consistency)\n        │\n        ▼\nSequential Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nCausal Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nRead-Your-Writes Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nMonotonic Read Consistency\n        │\n        ▼\nEventual Consistency\n\n                                                        LINEARIZABILITY(The Strongest — CAP's \"C\")\n\nThis is the absolute gold standard of consistency. It creates the perfect illusion of a single database.\n\nThe Core Rule: It is strictly bound to physical real-world time. Once a write operation is confirmed as successful, no client can ever read the \nold data again, regardless of which server on earth they query. Operations appear to execute instantaneously.\n\nThe Analogy (The Global Whiteboard): Imagine a whiteboard in New York magically linked to boards in Tokyo and London. If I change the New \nYork board from \"$100\" to \"$50\", nobody in Tokyo or London is allowed to look at their boards until the ink is perfectly dry on all three.\n\nThe Cost (The Latency Penalty): To achieve this, the primary node uses synchronous replication. It locks the record, sends the update to \nreplicas, and holds the user's connection open until all replicas confirm. My database's write speed is literally capped by the latency of the \nslowest, laggiest server in the cluster.\n\nThe Google Spanner Exception: Standard databases struggle to do this globally because physical server clocks naturally drift out of sync. \nGoogle solved this by building the TrueTime API. They installed atomic clocks and GPS receivers directly into their data center racks. \nTrueTime gives Spanner a guaranteed \"maximum uncertainty window\" (e.g., ±4 milliseconds). By intentionally pausing for those few milliseconds \nbefore returning a success response, Spanner mathematically guarantees a perfect global chronological order without the brutal latency penalties \nof cross-continental locking.\n\nWhen to use it: High-frequency stock trading, or any system where a millisecond difference in the real world dictates a massive financial outcome.\n\n                                                    SEQUENTIAL CONSISTENCY (The Pragmatic Transaction Model)\n\nThis model throws away the physical clock entirely. It does not matter when events actually occurred in the real world; it only matters that all \nnodes agree on a single, official sequence of events.\n\nThe Core Rule: As long as every process in the system agrees on the exact same numbered sequence of operations, the system is sequentially \nconsistent. Different nodes might lag behind real-time, but they will never process events out of the agreed-upon order.\n\nThe Analogy (The WhatsApp Group Chat): Alice asks who is driving. Bob and Charlie reply at the exact same real-world millisecond. The server \nreceives Charlie's packet first and decides the order is Alice → Charlie → Bob. Even if Dave has a terrible 3G connection and doesn't get the \nmessages for 10 seconds, his phone will display them in that exact top-to-bottom order. Everyone agrees on the history.\n\nHow it works without clocks: Instead of relying on timestamps, distributed systems use consensus algorithms like Raft or Paxos. These \nalgorithms force the nodes to vote and create an official ticket number for every transaction.\n\nWhy it is perfect for standard transactions: If a user makes a deposit and a withdrawal at roughly the same time, I don't need TrueTime to \nkeep the math safe. The consensus algorithm votes: Deposit is Ticket #1, Withdrawal is Ticket #2. Even if a specific replica is lagging and \nreceives the withdrawal network packet first, it looks at the ticket number and refuses to execute it until it receives and processes Deposit #1. \nIt guarantees \"First In, First Out\" based on the official list, fully protecting the ledger.\n\n                                                                                       CAUSAL CONSISTENCY\n\nIf operation A causally precedes operation B (A happened before B, or A's result influenced B), then all nodes see A before B. Unrelated opera-\ntions can be seen in any order.\n\nThe analogy: A comment thread. If User A posts a comment and User B replies to it, everyone should see User A's comment before User B's reply \n(because the reply causally depends on the original). But if User C posts an unrelated comment at the same time, it doesn't matter whether C's \ncomment appears before or after A's comment on different nodes.\n\nWhere it's used: Social media comment ordering. Git — commits that build on previous commits must appear in order. Google Docs collaborative \nediting — your edits that build on previous state must be applied in order.\n\nVector clocks (covered later in this distributed systems section) are the mechanism for tracking causality. They're what makes causal consistency \nimplementable.\n\n                                                                    READ-YOUR-WRITES CONSISTENCY\n\nAfter you write something, you always see your own write in subsequent reads. Other users might not see it yet (it's still propagating), but you \nalways see your own writes.\n\nThe analogy: You post a tweet. You immediately refresh and see your tweet. Your friend in another country might not see it yet — the data \nhasn't propagated to their closest server. But you always see your own tweets immediately.\n\nHow it's implemented:\n\nOption 1: Read from the same node you wrote to (sticky routing)\n    - Write goes to Server A\n    - All reads for this user go to Server A (until replication catches up)\n    - Problem: Server A becomes the bottleneck for this user\n\nOption 2: Track write timestamps\n    - After writing, client receives a \"write token\" with timestamp\n    - On reads, client sends the token\n    - Read replica checks: \"Is my replication lag less than this timestamp?\"\n    - If yes, serve from replica. If no, route to primary.\n\n                                                                       MONOTONIC READ CONSISTENCY\n\nOnce you've read a value, you'll never read an older value. You won't go backward in time.\n\nThe analogy: Reading a news website. You read the headline \"Stock market up 3%.\" If you refresh, you might see \"Stock market up 3.2%\" (newer) \nbut you'll never see \"Stock market up 2%\" (older than what you already saw).\n\nWithout monotonic reads: You could read from Replica A (latest data), then your next request goes to Replica B (which is slightly behind), and \nyou see older data. This creates a disorienting time-travel effect.\n\nHow it's implemented: Session-based routing — once a user reads from a specific replica, all subsequent reads for that session go to the same \nreplica (or a replica that's at least as up-to-date).\n\n\n                                                            EVENTUAL CONSISTENCY (The Weakest)\n                                                            \nIf no new writes occur, eventually all replicas will converge to the same value. But in the meantime, different replicas might return different values \nfor the same key.\n\nThe analogy: Airport departure boards. When a flight status changes, the update propagates from the central system to all displays — but for a \nbrief window, some displays might show \"On Time\" while others show \"Delayed.\" Eventually they all show the same thing.\n\nThe key property: \"Eventually\" has no time bound specified. It could be milliseconds, seconds, or minutes. The system makes no guarantee about \nHOW eventually.\n\nWhere it's acceptable:\n- DNS propagation (24-48 hours is acceptable)\n- Social media like counts (seeing 1,423 vs 1,424 likes doesn't matter)\n- Product recommendation caches (slightly stale recommendations are fine)\n- Shopping cart contents (debatable — Amazon famously uses eventual consistency for carts)\n- Analytics data (approximate counts are usually acceptable)\n\nWhere it's NOT acceptable:\n- Bank balances (must be consistent — can't show £1,000 to one teller and £500 to another)\n- Inventory counts (can't oversell because replicas showed different availability)\n- Authentication/authorization (can't let a revoked token still work on some nodes)\n- Financial ledgers (every transaction must be correctly sequenced)\n\n                                                              THE PRACTICAL DECISION FRAMEWORK\n\nIs incorrect data dangerous or just inconvenient?\n    Dangerous (money, auth, inventory) → Strong consistency (linearizability or sequential)\n    Inconvenient (stale cache, slightly old feed) → Eventual consistency\n\nWhat's the write frequency?\n    High-frequency writes, read-heavy → Eventual consistency (replicas can't keep up synchronously)\n    Low-frequency writes → Strong consistency is feasible\n\nWhat's your latency requirement?\n    Sub-10ms → Eventual consistency (synchronous replication adds too much latency)\n    100ms+ acceptable → Strong consistency is viable\n\nIs your data a social graph or interconnected relationships?\n    Yes → Causal consistency (replies must appear after their parent comment)\n    No → Simpler model sufficient\n\nDo users care about their own writes immediately?\n    Yes → At minimum, read-your-writes consistency\n\n\n                                                                    HOW THESE RELATE TO CAP\nCAP's \"C\" = Linearizability (the strongest model)\nCP systems → Linearizability or Sequential Consistency\nAP systems → Causal, Read-Your-Writes, Monotonic, or Eventual Consistency\n\nBut most real systems don't just pick one:\n- Cassandra: Eventual by default, tunable to Sequential with CONSISTENCY ALL\n- DynamoDB: Eventual by default, strong with ConsistentRead=true (costs 2x read units)\n- MongoDB: Eventual by default, strong with majority read concern",
